Hello Digital UNIX admins, 

Many thanks to these folks for responding to my question
about the missing acl.h file for the DU4.0B c compiler. 

Dr. Tom Blinn <tpb@zk3.dec.com>
Stephen LaBelle <labelles@mscd.edu>
Jonathan Rozes <jrozes@gumbo.tcs.tufts.edu>
Matti Saarinen <mjs@cc.tut.fi>
Per Ivar Emanuelsen <Per-Ivar.Emanuelsen@phys.uit.no>

According to Dr. Tom Blinn of DEC's UNIX Software Group,
there is apparently a bug in the DU 4.0B kit delivery: the
acl.h file that should be in the /usr/include directory is 
actually missing from the distributation.  He sent me a copy
of this missing acl.h and I have included it at the very
end of this message, AFTER my original post.  In the mean
time, his group at Digital has made correcting the problem 
a fairly high priority.

The other admins that responded indicated that they solved
the problem by using the acl.h file that is in the directory
/usr/sys/include/sys.  (The /usr/include/sys directory is
a soft link to /usr/sys/include/sys.)  However, Tom Blinn
said that this acl.h file is probably NOT the correct one.
Like the other sys admins, I find that this "alternate" acl.h
seems to work okay (for my purposes), but I have decided
to use the one Dr Blinn has provided.  If you want to use
the "alternate" acl.h found in /usr/sys/include/sys, I
received three suggestions:
(1) Change the reference in /usr/include/prot.h from
    <acl.h> to <sys/acl.h>
    This solution from Jonathan Rozes.
(2) Copy acl.h from /usr/sys/incluse/sys to /usr/include
    This from Stephen LaBelle and Matti Saarinen.
    (I tried this one -- it worked for me.)
(3) Make a symbolic link to /usr/sys/include/sys/acl.h 
    in /usr/include:
    ln -s /usr/include/sys/acl.h /usr/include/acl.h
    This from Per-Ivar Emanuelsen. 

Paul Youngblood

